1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,903
Lesson 5.2.

2
00:00:03,283 --> 00:00:04,949
p-bits: MTJ and CMOS.

3
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:09,288
In unit four the pbit was
an abstraction: a spin

4
00:00:09,288 --> 00:00:12,392
that equals plus one
with sigmoid probability.

5
00:00:12,772 --> 00:00:14,576
Now let's see how it is built.

6
00:00:14,956 --> 00:00:15,918
There are two paths.

7
00:00:16,298 --> 00:00:21,023
One is physical: a magnet so tiny and
unstable that heat flips it on its own.

8
00:00:21,403 --> 00:00:26,898
The other is digital: a random generator,
a table and a comparator on an FPGA.

9
00:00:27,278 --> 00:00:29,240
Both lead to p-computers.

10
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:34,936
A magnetic tunnel junction has two
magnetic layers separated by an insulator.

11
00:00:35,316 --> 00:00:38,072
If the magnetizations are parallel, the

12
00:00:38,072 --> 00:00:40,969
resistance is low; if antiparallel, high.

13
00:00:41,349 --> 00:00:44,738
In a magnetic memory, the
free layer has a barrier of

14
00:00:44,738 --> 00:00:48,128
about forty k_B T so that
it does not flip for years.

15
00:00:48,508 --> 00:00:52,582
In a pbit we do the opposite: a
free layer with a barrier on the

16
00:00:52,582 --> 00:00:56,466
order of k_B T, which thermal
noise flips within nanoseconds.

17
00:00:56,846 --> 00:01:00,660
We read the resistance, and
its voltage is a random bit.

18
00:01:01,783 --> 00:01:07,285
The mean time between flips
follows the Néel–Arrhenius law, τ =

19
00:01:07,285 --> 00:01:11,913
τ₀ e^(Δ/k_B T), with τ₀ on
the order of a nanosecond.

20
00:01:12,293 --> 00:01:15,306
With a barrier of forty
k_B T, the magnet stays

21
00:01:15,306 --> 00:01:17,998
put for about seven years: it is a memory.

22
00:01:18,378 --> 00:01:20,082
With twenty, half a second.

23
00:01:20,462 --> 00:01:24,424
With a single k_B T, less than
three nanoseconds: it is a pbit.

24
00:01:24,804 --> 00:01:27,436
The same device goes from memory to random

25
00:01:27,436 --> 00:01:30,318
generator just by changing
the barrier height.

26
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:35,147
To be a pbit, the input must
control the probability.

27
00:01:35,527 --> 00:01:40,726
A current or voltage tilts the double
well: it favors one state over the other.

28
00:01:41,106 --> 00:01:45,078
The fraction of time in the high
state follows a sigmoid of the input.

29
00:01:45,458 --> 00:01:49,115
In the typical circuit, the junction
is connected to a transistor

30
00:01:49,115 --> 00:01:52,716
and an inverter: the input is the
gate voltage and the output is

31
00:01:52,716 --> 00:01:55,586
a bit that fluctuates with
the desired probability.

32
00:01:55,967 --> 00:02:00,723
The jump rates, and therefore the
speed, stay in the nanoseconds.

33
00:02:01,850 --> 00:02:05,926
The digital path emulates the same
behavior with standard circuits.

34
00:02:06,306 --> 00:02:12,222
A pseudorandom generator produces a number,
a table stores the hyperbolic tangent,

35
00:02:12,222 --> 00:02:17,488
and a comparator decides the sign,
exactly the rule we saw in lesson 4.5.

36
00:02:17,868 --> 00:02:22,484
It does not save the energy of randomness,
but it can be built today on an FPGA,

37
00:02:22,484 --> 00:02:26,523
with hundreds of thousands of pbits
and whatever connectivity we want.

38
00:02:26,903 --> 00:02:30,191
It is the platform on which
p-computers were developed.

39
00:02:31,317 --> 00:02:34,174
A p-computer connects
many pbits according to a

40
00:02:34,174 --> 00:02:36,850
graph and updates them
with chromatic Gibbs.

41
00:02:37,230 --> 00:02:41,137
The key to its speed is
sparsity: if each pbit has few

42
00:02:41,137 --> 00:02:45,478
neighbors, the graph is colored
with few colors, and in each

43
00:02:45,478 --> 00:02:49,385
clock phase all pbits of one
color update in parallel.

44
00:02:49,765 --> 00:02:55,253
Sparse Ising machines on FPGA reach
billions of flips per second, several

45
00:02:55,253 --> 00:03:00,591
orders of magnitude more than a CPU
going through the spins one by one.

46
00:03:01,717 --> 00:03:06,373
Stochastic tunnel junctions give
nanosecond flips and tunable probability,

47
00:03:06,373 --> 00:03:10,841
although each device is somewhat
different and they must be calibrated.

48
00:03:11,221 --> 00:03:14,771
FPGA p-computers already
solve optimization,

49
00:03:14,771 --> 00:03:17,919
sampling and invertible-logic problems.

50
00:03:18,298 --> 00:03:23,414
In lab eight you will simulate pbit
circuits and measure their transfer curve.

51
00:03:24,517 --> 00:03:28,484
Common mistakes: using a memory
junction, which never flips.

52
00:03:28,864 --> 00:03:31,413
Ignoring device-to-device variability.

53
00:03:31,793 --> 00:03:33,903
Updating neighbors in the same phase.

54
00:03:34,284 --> 00:03:36,819
And counting flips per
second without checking

55
00:03:36,819 --> 00:03:39,079
that the sampled distribution is correct.

56
00:03:40,183 --> 00:03:44,180
In summary: a magnetic tunnel
junction with a barrier on the order

57
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:47,996
of k_B T is a physical pbit that
fluctuates within nanoseconds,

58
00:03:47,996 --> 00:03:51,326
and an input that tilts its
well tunes the probability.

59
00:03:51,706 --> 00:03:55,229
A digital pbit combines a
generator, a table and a comparator.

60
00:03:55,609 --> 00:04:00,081
Connected in sparse, colored
graphs, they form fast p-computers.

61
00:04:01,183 --> 00:04:05,019
To practice: compute the
barrier for a given flip time.

62
00:04:05,399 --> 00:04:08,320
Compare the barriers
of a pbit and a memory.

63
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:10,671
Count the colors of a square lattice.

64
00:04:11,051 --> 00:04:15,169
And explain what a digital pbit spends
energy on that a magnetic one does not.
